.SH NAME
Time::Piece \- Object Oriented time objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&    use Time::Piece;
\&
\&    my $t = localtime;
\&    print "Time is $t\en";
\&    print "Year is ", $t\->year, "\en";
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This module replaces the standard \f(CW\*(C`localtime\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`gmtime\*(C'\fR functions with
implementations that return objects. It does so in a backwards
compatible manner, so that using localtime/gmtime in the way documented
in perlfunc will still return what you expect.
.PP
The module actually implements most of an interface described by
Larry Wall on the perl5\-porters mailing list here:
<https://www.nntp.perl.org/group/perl.perl5.porters/2000/01/msg5283.html>
.PP
After importing this module, when you use \f(CW\*(C`localtime\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`gmtime\*(C'\fR in a scalar
context, rather than getting an ordinary scalar string representing the
date and time, you get a \f(CW\*(C`Time::Piece\*(C'\fR object, whose stringification happens
to produce the same effect as the \f(CW\*(C`localtime\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`gmtime\*(C'\fR functions.
.PP
The primary way to create Time::Piece objects is through the \f(CW\*(C`localtime\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`gmtime\*(C'\fR functions. There is also a \f(CWnew()\fR constructor which is the same as
\&\f(CWlocaltime()\fR, except when passed a Time::Piece object, in which case it\*(Aqs a
copy constructor.
.SH "Public Methods"
.IX Header "Public Methods"
The following methods are available on the object:
.SS "Time Components"
.IX Subsection "Time Components"
.Vb 3
\&    $t\->sec                 # also available as $t\->second
\&    $t\->min                 # also available as $t\->minute
\&    $t\->hour                # 24 hour
.Ve
.SS "Date Components"
.IX Subsection "Date Components"
.Vb 6
\&    $t\->mday                # also available as $t\->day_of_month
\&    $t\->mon                 # 1 = January
\&    $t\->_mon                # 0 = January
\&    $t\->year                # based at 0 (year 0 AD is, of course 1 BC)
\&    $t\->_year               # year minus 1900
\&    $t\->yy                  # 2 digit year
.Ve
.SS "Day and Month Names"
.IX Subsection "Day and Month Names"
.Vb 9
\&    $t\->monname             # Feb
\&    $t\->month               # same as $t\->monname
\&    $t\->fullmonth           # February
\&    $t\->wday                # 1 = Sunday
\&    $t\->_wday               # 0 = Sunday
\&    $t\->day_of_week         # 0 = Sunday
\&    $t\->wdayname            # Tue
\&    $t\->day                 # same as wdayname
\&    $t\->fullday             # Tuesday
.Ve
.SS "Formatted Date/Time Output"
.IX Subsection "Formatted Date/Time Output"
.Vb 10
\&    $t\->hms                 # 12:34:56
\&    $t\->hms(".")            # 12.34.56
\&    $t\->time                # same as $t\->hms
\&    $t\->ymd                 # 2000\-02\-29
\&    $t\->date                # same as $t\->ymd
\&    $t\->mdy                 # 02\-29\-2000
\&    $t\->mdy("/")            # 02/29/2000
\&    $t\->dmy                 # 29\-02\-2000
\&    $t\->dmy(".")            # 29.02.2000
\&    $t\->datetime            # 2000\-02\-29T12:34:56 (ISO 8601)
\&    $t\->cdate               # Tue Feb 29 12:34:56 2000
\&    "$t"                    # same as $t\->cdate
\&    $t\->strftime(FORMAT)    # same as POSIX::strftime (without the overhead
\&                            # of the full POSIX extension)
\&    $t\->strftime()          # "Tue, 29 Feb 2000 12:34:56 GMT"
.Ve
.PP
\fIstrftime Format Flags\fR
.IX Subsection "strftime Format Flags"
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`strftime\*(C'\fR method calls your system\*(Aqs native \f(CWstrftime()\fR implementation,
so the supported format flags and their behavior will depend on your platform.
.PP
\&\fBPlatform Variability:\fR Some format flags behave differently or may be missing
entirely on certain platforms. The following flags are known to have
platform\-specific issues: \f(CW%e\fR, \f(CW%D\fR, \f(CW%F\fR, \f(CW%k\fR, \f(CW%l\fR, \f(CW%P\fR, \f(CW%r\fR, \f(CW%R\fR,
\&\f(CW%s\fR, \f(CW%T\fR, \f(CW%u\fR, \f(CW%V\fR, \f(CW%z\fR, and \f(CW%Z\fR.
.PP
To mitigate these differences, \f(CW\*(C`Time::Piece\*(C'\fR includes a special translation layer
that attempts to unify behavior across platforms. For example, \f(CW%F\fR is not
available on some Microsoft platforms, so it is automatically converted to
\&\f(CW"%Y\-%m\-%d"\fR internally before calling the system\*(Aqs \f(CWstrftime()\fR.
.PP
For a complete list of format flags supported by your system, consult your
platform\*(Aqs \f(CWstrftime(3)\fR manual page (\f(CW\*(C`man strftime\*(C'\fR on Unix\-like systems).
.SS "Epoch and Calendar Calculations"
.IX Subsection "Epoch and Calendar Calculations"
.Vb 5
\&    $t\->epoch               # seconds since the epoch
\&    $t\->julian_day          # number of days since Julian period began
\&    $t\->mjd                 # modified Julian date (JD\-2400000.5 days)
\&    $t\->week                # week number (ISO 8601)
\&    $t\->yday                # also available as $t\->day_of_year, 0 = Jan 01
.Ve
.SS "Timezone and DST"
.IX Subsection "Timezone and DST"
.Vb 4
\&    $t\->tzoffset            # timezone offset in a Time::Seconds object
\&    $t\->isdst               # also available as $t\->daylight_savings
\&    $t\->to_gmtime           # convert to GMT, preserving the epoch
\&    $t\->to_localtime        # convert to local time, preserving the epoch
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`isdst\*(C'\fR method returns:
.IP \(bu 4
0 for GMT/UTC times (they never have DST)
.IP \(bu 4
0 or 1 for local times depending on whether DST is active
.IP \(bu 4
Automatically calculated if unknown
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`tzoffset\*(C'\fR method returns the offset from UTC as a Time::Seconds object.
For GMT/UTC times, this always returns 0. For local times, it calculates
the actual offset including any DST adjustment.
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`to_gmtime\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`to_localtime\*(C'\fR methods convert between timezone contexts
while preserving the same moment in time (epoch). They always return a new
Time::Piece object.
.SS "Utility Methods"
.IX Subsection "Utility Methods"
.Vb 5
\&    $t\->is_leap_year        # true if it\*(Aqs a leap year
\&    $t\->month_last_day      # 28\-31
\&    $t\->add_days            # Add days
\&    $t\->add_months          # Add months
\&    $t\->add_years           # Add years
.Ve
.SS "Global Configuration"
.IX Subsection "Global Configuration"
.Vb 6
\&    $t\->time_separator($s)     # set the default separator (default ":")
\&    $t\->date_separator($s)     # set the default separator (default "\-")
\&    $t\->day_list(@days)        # set the names used by wdayname()
\&    $t\->mon_list(@months)      # set the names used by month()
\&    $t\->fullday_list(@days)    # set the names used by fullday()
\&    $t\->fullmon_list(@months)  # set the names used by fullmonth()
.Ve
.SS Parsing
.IX Subsection "Parsing"
.Vb 3
\&    Time::Piece\->strptime(STRING, FORMAT)
\&                            # see strptime man page. Creates a new
\&                            # Time::Piece object
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR \f(CW\*(C`localtime\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`gmtime\*(C'\fR are not listed above. If called as
methods on a Time::Piece object, they act as constructors, returning a new
Time::Piece object for the current time. In other words: they\*(Aqre not useful as
methods.
.SH "Date Calculations"
.IX Header "Date Calculations"
It\*(Aqs possible to use simple addition and subtraction of objects:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Time::Seconds;
\&
\&    my $seconds = $t1 \- $t2;
\&    $t1 += ONE_DAY; # add 1 day (constant from Time::Seconds)
.Ve
.PP
The following are valid ($t1 and \f(CW$t2\fR are Time::Piece objects):
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    $t1 \- $t2; # returns Time::Seconds object
\&    $t1 \- 42; # returns Time::Piece object
\&    $t1 + 533; # returns Time::Piece object
\&    $t1\->add_days(2); # returns Time::Piece object
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR All arithmetic uses epoch seconds (UTC). When daylight saving time
(DST) changes occur:
.IP \(bu 4
Adding seconds works on UTC time, so adding 3600 seconds during DST
transition from 1:30 AM gives 3:30 AM (not 2:30 AM, which doesn\*(Aqt exist
during "spring forward")
.IP \(bu 4
Subtracting across DST transitions may differ from wall\-clock expectations
due to skipped or repeated hours
.SS "Adding Months and Years"
.IX Subsection "Adding Months and Years"
Two methods handle calendar arithmetic differently than seconds\-based math:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    $t = $t\->add_months(6);
\&    $t = $t\->add_years(5);
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBImportant behaviors:\fR
.IP \(bu 4
These preserve the day\-of\-month number, which can cause overflow (Jan 31 + 1
month = Mar 3, since "Feb 31" doesn\*(Aqt exist)
.IP \(bu 4
Wall\-clock time is preserved across DST transitions
.IP \(bu 4
Order matters: \f(CWadd_months(1)\fR then \f(CW\*(C`+ 86400\*(C'\fR gives different results than
\&\f(CW\*(C`+ 86400\*(C'\fR then \f(CWadd_months(1)\fR
.SH Truncation
.IX Header "Truncation"
Calling the \f(CW\*(C`truncate\*(C'\fR method returns a copy of the object but with the
time truncated to the start of the supplied unit.
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    $t = $t\->truncate(to => \*(Aqday\*(Aq);
.Ve
.PP
This example will set the time to midnight on the same date which \f(CW$t\fR
had previously. Allowed values for the "to" parameter are: "year",
"quarter", "month", "day", "hour", "minute" and "second".
.SH "Date Comparisons"
.IX Header "Date Comparisons"
Date comparisons are also possible, using the full suite of "<", ">",
"<=", ">=", "<=>", "==" and "!=".
.PP
All comparisons use epoch seconds, so they work correctly across timezones:
.PP
.Vb 5
\&    my $t1 = localtime;
\&    my $t2 = gmtime;
\&    if ($t1 > $t2) {  # Compares actual moments in time, not clock values
\&        # ...
\&    }
.Ve
.PP
Time::Piece objects can also be compared as strings using \f(CW\*(C`cmp\*(C'\fR:
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    if ($t1 cmp "2024\-01\-15") {  # Compares against cdate format
\&        # ...
\&    }
.Ve
.SH "Date Parsing"
.IX Header "Date Parsing"
Time::Piece provides flexible date parsing via the built\-in \f(CWstrptime()\fR
function (from FreeBSD).
.PP
For more information on acceptible formats and flags for \f(CW\*(C`strptime\*(C'\fR see
"man strptime" on unix systems. Alternatively look here:
<http://www.unix.com/man\-page/FreeBSD/3/strftime/>
.SS "Basic Usage"
.IX Subsection "Basic Usage"
.Vb 2
\&  my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("Sunday 3rd Nov, 1943",
\&                                "%A %drd %b, %Y");
\&
\&  print $t\->strftime("%a, %d %b %Y");
.Ve
.PP
Outputs:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&  Wed, 03 Nov 1943
.Ve
.PP
The default format string is \f(CW"%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %Z"\fR, so these are equivalent:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    my $t1 = Time::Piece\->strptime($string);
\&    my $t2 = Time::Piece\->strptime($string, "%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S %Z");
.Ve
.SS "Supported Format Flags"
.IX Subsection "Supported Format Flags"
\&\f(CW\*(C`Time::Piece\*(C'\fR uses a custom \f(CWstrptime()\fR implementation that supports the
following format flags:
.PP
.Vb 10
\&    Flag  Description
\&    \-\-\-\-  \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\&    %%    Literal \*(Aq%\*(Aq character
\&    %a    Abbreviated weekday name (Mon, Tue, etc.)
\&    %A    Full weekday name (Monday, Tuesday, etc.)
\&    %b    Abbreviated month name (Jan, Feb, etc.)
\&    %B    Full month name (January, February, etc.)
\&    %C    Century number (00\-99)
\&    %d    Day of month (01\-31)
\&    %D    Equivalent to %m/%d/%y
\&    %e    Day of month ( 1\-31, space\-padded)
\&    %f    Fractional seconds as microseconds (up to 6 digits, parsed but ignored)
\&    %F    Equivalent to %Y\-%m\-%d (ISO 8601 date format)
\&    %h    Abbreviated month name (same as %b)
\&    %H    Hour in 24\-hour format (00\-23)
\&    %I    Hour in 12\-hour format (01\-12)
\&    %j    Day of year (001\-366)
\&    %k    Hour in 24\-hour format ( 0\-23, space\-padded)
\&    %l    Hour in 12\-hour format ( 1\-12, space\-padded)
\&    %m    Month number (01\-12)
\&    %M    Minute (00\-59)
\&    %n    Any whitespace
\&    %p    AM/PM indicator
\&    %P    Alt AM/PM indicator
\&    %r    Time in AM/PM format (%I:%M:%S %p, or %H:%M:%S if locale has no AM/PM)
\&    %R    Equivalent to %H:%M
\&    %s    Seconds since Unix epoch (1970\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC)
\&    %S    Second (00\-60, allowing for leap seconds)
\&    %t    Any whitespace (same as %n)
\&    %T    Equivalent to %H:%M:%S
\&    %u    Weekday as number (1\-7, Monday = 1)
\&    %w    Weekday as number (0\-6, Sunday = 0)
\&    %y    Year within century (00\-99). Values 00\-68 are 2000\-2068, 69\-99 are 1969\-1999
\&    %Y    Year with century (e.g., 2024)
\&    %z    Timezone offset (+HHMM, \-HHMM, +HH:MM, or \-HH:MM)
\&    %Z    Timezone name (only GMT and UTC recognized; others parsed but ignored)
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBUnsupported Locale Flags:\fR The format flags \f(CW%c\fR, \f(CW%x\fR, and \f(CW%X\fR are \fBnot\fR
supported as they are highly locale\-dependent and have inconsistent formats
across systems. However, you can construct equivalent formats using the individual
flags listed above. For example, \f(CW%c\fR is typically equivalent to something like:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    "%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y"   # e.g., "Tue Feb 29 12:34:56 2000"
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR \f(CW%U\fR, \f(CW%V\fR, and \f(CW%W\fR (week number formats) are parsed but not fully
implemented in the strptime logic, as they require additional date components
to calculate the actual date.
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR \f(CW%f\fR (fractional seconds) is only supported in \f(CW\*(C`strptime\*(C'\fR for parsing.
It is not available in \f(CW\*(C`strftime\*(C'\fR for output formatting, as Time::Piece uses
epoch seconds which do not store subsecond precision.
.SS "GMT vs Local Time"
.IX Subsection "GMT vs Local Time"
By default, \f(CW\*(C`strptime\*(C'\fR returns GMT objects when called as a class method:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    # Returns GMT (c_islocal = 0)
\&    Time::Piece\->strptime($string, $format)
.Ve
.PP
To get local time objects, you can:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    # Call as instance method on localtime object
\&    localtime()\->strptime($string, $format)
\&
\&    # Use explicit islocal option
\&    Time::Piece\->strptime($string, $format, { islocal => 1 })
\&
\&    # Pass a local Time::Piece object as defaults
\&    my $local = localtime();
\&    Time::Piece\->strptime($string, $format, { defaults => $local })
.Ve
.PP
The islocal and defaults options were added in version 1.37; the instance
method can be used for compatibility with previous versions.
.ie n .SS "Timezone Parsing with %z and %Z"
.el .SS "Timezone Parsing with \f(CW%z\fP and \f(CW%Z\fP"
.IX Subsection "Timezone Parsing with %z and %Z"
Time::Piece\*(Aqs \f(CWstrptime()\fR function has some limited support for parsing timezone
information through two format specifiers: \f(CW%z\fR and \f(CW%Z\fR
.PP
Added in version 1.38. Prior to that, these flags were mostly ignored.
Consider the current implementation somewhat "alpha" and in need of feedback.
.PP
\fINumeric Offsets (%z)\fR
.IX Subsection "Numeric Offsets (%z)"
.PP
The \f(CW%z\fR specifier parses numeric timezone offsets
(format: \f(CW\*(C`[+\-]HHMM\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`[+\-]HH:MM\*(C'\fR, or \f(CW\*(C`[+\-]HH\*(C'\fR):
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("2024\-01\-15 15:30:00 +0500",
\&                                  "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %z");
\&    print $t\->hour;  # prints 10 (converted to UTC: 15:30 \- 5:00)
.Ve
.PP
Key behaviors:
.IP \(bu 4
Offsets are applied to convert to UTC (\f(CW+0500\fR means "5 hours ahead of UTC")
.IP \(bu 4
Valid range: \f(CW\-1200\fR to \f(CW+1400\fR with minutes less than 60
.IP \(bu 4
For local objects (\f(CW\*(C`islocal == 1\*(C'\fR), the result is converted to system timezone
.PP
Times parsed with timezone information default to GMT. To convert to local time:
.PP
.Vb 5
\&    # Parse and convert to local timezone
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("2024\-01\-15 15:30:00 +0500",
\&                                  "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %z",
\&                                  { islocal => 1 });
\&    # Result: 10:30 UTC converted to your local timezone
.Ve
.PP
\fITimezone Names (%Z)\fR
.IX Subsection "Timezone Names (%Z)"
.PP
The \f(CW%Z\fR specifier currently only recognizes "GMT" and "UTC" (case\-sensitive).
Other timezone names are parsed \fBbut ignored\fR:
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    # GMT/UTC recognized and handled
\&    my $t1 = Time::Piece\->strptime("2024\-01\-15 10:30:00 GMT",
\&                                   "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z");
\&    print $t1\->hour;  # prints 10 (no adjustment)
\&
\&    # Other timezones parsed but ignored
\&    my $t2 = Time::Piece\->strptime("2024\-01\-15 10:30:00 PST",
\&                                   "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z");
\&    print $t2\->hour;  # prints 10 (PST ignored \- no adjustment)
\&
\&    # Parse and convert to local timezone
\&    my $t3 = Time::Piece\->strptime("2024\-01\-15 15:30:00 UTC",
\&                                  "%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z",
\&                                  { islocal => 1 });
\&    print $t3\->hour;  # prints 10:30 UTC converted to your local timezone
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR Full timezone name support is not currently implemented. For reliable
timezone handling beyond GMT/UTC, consider using the DateTime module.
.SS "Handling Partial Dates"
.IX Subsection "Handling Partial Dates"
When parsing incomplete date strings, you can provide defaults for missing
components in several ways:
.PP
\&\fBArray Reference\fR \- Standard time components (as returned by localtime):
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    my @defaults = localtime();
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("15 Mar", "%d %b",
\&                                  { defaults => \e@defaults });
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBHash Reference\fR \- Specify only needed components:
.PP
.Vb 6
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("15 Mar", "%d %b",
\&                                  { defaults => {
\&                                      year => 2023,
\&                                      hour => 14,
\&                                      min  => 30
\&                                  } });
.Ve
.PP
Valid keys: \f(CW\*(C`sec\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`hour\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mday\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mon\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`year\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`wday\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`yday\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`isdst\*(C'\fR
.PP
\&\fBNote\fR: For the \f(CW\*(C`year\*(C'\fR parameter numbers less than 1000 are treated as an
offset from 1900. Whereas numbers larger than 1000 are treated as the actual year.
.PP
\&\fBTime::Piece Object\fR \- Uses all components from the object:
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    my $base = localtime();
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("15 Mar", "%d %b",
\&                                  { defaults => $base });
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR In all cases, parsed values always override defaults. Only missing
components use default values.
.SS "Locale Considerations"
.IX Subsection "Locale Considerations"
By default, \f(CW\*(C`strptime\*(C'\fR only parses English day and month names, while
\&\f(CW\*(C`strftime\*(C'\fR uses your system locale. This can cause parsing failures for
non\-English dates.
.PP
To parse localized dates, call \f(CW\*(C`Time::Piece\->use_locale()\*(C'\fR to build
a list of your locale\*(Aqs day and month names:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&    # Enable locale\-aware parsing (global setting)
\&    Time::Piece\->use_locale();
\&
\&    # Now strptime can parse names in your system locale
\&    my $t = Time::Piece\->strptime("15 Marzo 2024", "%d %B %Y");
.Ve
.PP
\&\fBNote:\fR This is a global change affecting all Time::Piece instances.
.PP
You can also override the day/month names manually:
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    # Abbreviated day names
\&    my @days = qw( Dom Lun Mar Mie Jue Vie Sab );
\&    my $spanish_day = localtime\->day(@days);
\&
\&    # Full day names
\&    my @fulldays = qw( Domingo Lunes Martes Miercoles Jueves Viernes Sabado );
\&    my $spanish_fullday = localtime\->fullday(@fulldays);
\&
\&    # Abbreviated month names
\&    my @months = qw( Ene Feb Mar Abr May Jun Jul Ago Sep Oct Nov Dic );
\&    print localtime\->month(@months);
\&
\&    # Full month names
\&    my @fullmonths = qw( Enero Febrero Marzo Abril Mayo Junio
\&                         Julio Agosto Septiembre Octubre Noviembre Diciembre );
\&    print localtime\->fullmonth(@fullmonths);
.Ve
.PP
Set globally with:
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    Time::Piece::day_list(@days);
\&    Time::Piece::mon_list(@months);
\&    Time::Piece::fullday_list(@fulldays);
\&    Time::Piece::fullmon_list(@fullmonths);
.Ve
.SH "Global Overriding"
.IX Header "Global Overriding"
To override localtime and gmtime everywhere:
.PP
.Vb 1
\&    use Time::Piece \*(Aq:override\*(Aq;
.Ve
.PP
This replaces Perl\*(Aqs built\-in functions with Time::Piece versions globally.
.SH CAVEATS
.IX Header "CAVEATS"
.ie n .SS "Setting $ENV{TZ} in Threads on Win32"
.el .SS "Setting \f(CW$ENV\fP{TZ} in Threads on Win32"
.IX Subsection "Setting $ENV{TZ} in Threads on Win32"
Note that when using perl in the default build configuration on Win32
(specifically, when perl is built with PERL_IMPLICIT_SYS), each perl
interpreter maintains its own copy of the environment and only the main
interpreter will update the process environment seen by strftime.
.PP
Therefore, if you make changes to \f(CW$ENV\fR{TZ} from inside a thread other than
the main thread then those changes will not be seen by \f(CW\*(C`strftime\*(C'\fR if you
subsequently call that with the \f(CW%Z\fR formatting code. You must change \f(CW$ENV\fR{TZ}
in the main thread to have the desired effect in this case (and you must
also call \f(CW_tzset()\fR in the main thread to register the environment change).
.PP
Furthermore, remember that this caveat also applies to \fBfork()\fR, which is
emulated by threads on Win32.
.SS "Use of epoch seconds"
.IX Subsection "Use of epoch seconds"
This module internally uses the epoch seconds system that is provided via
the perl \f(CWtime()\fR function and supported by \f(CWgmtime()\fR and \f(CWlocaltime()\fR.
.PP
If your perl does not support times larger than \f(CW\*(C`2^31\*(C'\fR seconds
(Perl versions < 5.12) then this module is likely to fail at processing dates
beyond the year 2038. If that is not an option, use the DateTime module
which has support for years well into the future and past.
